-- concept_book_prompts.spl — shared LLM prompt templates for concept-book generation.
--
-- Imported by build_concept_book.spl, build_concept_book_cache.spl, and
-- build_concept_only.spl:
--
--   IMPORT 'concept_book_prompts.spl'
--
-- Contains only CREATE FUNCTION definitions (no WORKFLOW, no state).

CREATE FUNCTION write_section(concept TEXT, label TEXT, context TEXT, style_guide TEXT, language TEXT, domain TEXT)
RETURN TEXT
AS $$
You are an expert author writing a concept-book section.

SUBJECT: Chinese characters and vocabulary — learning to read, write, and
understand hanzi (their meaning, structure, and how simpler characters
combine into compound characters, words, and phrases). This is a language
and literacy subject, not mathematics or any other STEM field. Never
introduce mathematical notation, equations, formulas, algorithms, or
STEM-domain analogies (e.g. baking-as-algorithm, "worked example" as an
equation) — every example, analogy, and worked illustration must stay
entirely within language, characters, words, and everyday communication.

{style_guide}

Domain: {domain}
Write a self-contained section for the concept: {concept}
Surrounding context: {context}

This section is cached and reused verbatim wherever "{concept}" is taught
again, in any other domain — treat {domain} as ONE illustrative example only,
never as a dependency. Explain {concept} on its own terms so the section
reads correctly even when a reader has never heard of {domain}. Do not build
the core explanation around {domain}'s specific story or meaning; you may
reference it briefly for color, but the section must stand alone.

Begin the section with a level-2 Markdown heading: ## {label}

Follow the structure and length specified in the STYLE GUIDE above. The
STRUCTURE labels there (e.g. "Picture this", "Worked example") describe what
each part should contain — they are English labels for your reference only,
not text to copy verbatim; "Worked example" here means a worked example of
*using the character/word/phrase in a sentence or real communication*, never
a mathematical worked example. Write every word of the output, including any
structural headers, entirely in {language}.
Output only the section text. No preamble, no metadata.

If the context includes a [Diagram: type — hint], generate a diagram
immediately after the main explanation (before any closing summary). Choose
whichever option better serves the hint:

OPTION A — Mermaid diagram (preferred for flows, DAGs, sequences,
comparisons, or any diagram that can be expressed in text-based syntax):
Output a fenced mermaid block, immediately followed by a brief italic caption.
Use the stated diagram type as the Mermaid diagram kind (e.g. "graph TD",
"flowchart LR", "sequenceDiagram"). Keep it to ≤12 nodes. Write all node
labels in {language}. Use valid Mermaid syntax only; no self-loops.
```mermaid
<diagram type>
<diagram body>
```
*Caption in {language} describing what the diagram shows.*

OPTION B — Public-domain image reference (preferred for molecular structures,
anatomical figures, or any diagram requiring visual/spatial precision that
Mermaid cannot represent):
Output exactly this HTML block (no surrounding Markdown, no extra tags):
<figure class="cb-figure">
  <img src="IMAGE_URL" alt="ALT_TEXT" class="cb-figure-img">
  <figcaption>CAPTION_IN_{language}. Source: <a href="SOURCE_URL">TITLE</a>, LICENSE.</figcaption>
</figure>
Use only images with verifiable, permanent URLs from Wikimedia Commons
(upload.wikimedia.org/…), OpenStax (openstax.org/…), RCSB PDB (rcsb.org/…),
or NCBI (ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/…). If you cannot identify a specific, real URL
for a public-domain image that directly illustrates this concept, choose
Option A instead. Never fabricate or guess a URL.

If neither option produces a useful diagram for this concept, omit it entirely.
Output only the section text. No preamble, no metadata.
$$;

CREATE FUNCTION refine_section(section TEXT, feedback TEXT, style_guide TEXT, domain TEXT, language TEXT)
RETURN TEXT
AS $$
You are an expert author. Revise the section below based on the feedback
provided. Keep all correct content; fix only what is flagged. Maintain the
style described below throughout the revision.

SUBJECT: Chinese characters and vocabulary — a language/literacy subject, not
mathematics or STEM. If the section below contains any mathematical
notation, equations, formulas, or STEM-domain analogies, remove them and
replace with language/character-appropriate illustrations — that is always
in scope for this revision, whether or not the feedback mentions it.

{style_guide}

Domain: {domain}
This section is cached and reused wherever this concept is taught again, in
any other domain — keep it a self-contained explanation of the concept
itself, not dependent on {domain}'s specific story or meaning.
The STRUCTURE labels in the STYLE GUIDE above are English labels for
reference only, not text to copy verbatim. Write every word of the revision,
including any structural headers, entirely in {language}.

SECTION:
{section}

FEEDBACK:
{feedback}

Output the revised section only. No preamble. No commentary.
$$;

CREATE FUNCTION write_payoff(target_label TEXT, applications TEXT, style_guide TEXT, domain TEXT, language TEXT)
RETURN TEXT
AS $$
You are an expert author writing the capstone of a concept-book.

SUBJECT: Chinese characters and vocabulary — a language/literacy subject, not
mathematics or STEM. Never introduce mathematical notation, equations,
formulas, or STEM-domain analogies; every illustration must stay within
language, characters, words, and everyday communication.

{style_guide}

Domain: {domain}
The textbook culminates in the concept: {target_label}
Applications this concept unlocks: {applications}

Write a "Payoff" section that:
1. Explains what {target_label} achieves and why it is the natural endpoint.
2. Shows how it connects to each listed application domain.
3. Ends with an invitation to explore one application in depth.

Begin with: ## Payoff

Follow the length and structure in the STYLE GUIDE above; any English
structure labels there describe the content only, not text to copy verbatim.
Write every word of the output, including any structural headers, entirely
in {language}.
Output only the section. No preamble.
$$;
